-- title:
--   Isomorphic targets give kernel ideals differing by a unit
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic a prime $q'$, and let $X_0$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ such that the only point $P$ of the affine curve $X_0$ over $\kappa$ with $q' \cdot P = 0$ is $0$. Let $a,b \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $a<0$ and $b<0$, and let $\theta$ be an injective ring homomorphism from [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalEndSubring κ X₀`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L32) — the subring of $\operatorname{End}$ of the additive group of affine points of $X_0$ base changed to $\kappa$ generated by those additive endomorphisms that are zero or given, away from a finite set of $x$-coordinates, by ratios of bivariate polynomials over $\kappa$ in both coordinates — into the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$. Let $W, W'$ be elliptic Weierstrass curves over $\kappa$, and let $\chi$, resp. $\chi'$, be a nonzero additive homomorphism from the affine points of $X_0$ to those of $W$, resp. $W'$, after base change to $\kappa$, each lying in the corresponding set of rationally represented homomorphisms (zero, or given by such ratios of polynomials off a finite set). Assume $W' = \gamma \cdot W$ for some Weierstrass variable change $\gamma$ over $\kappa$. Then there is a unit $c$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ such that the $\theta$-image of $\{\rho \circ \chi' : \rho \in \operatorname{Hom}^{\mathrm{rat}}(W',X_0)\}$ equals the $\theta$-image of $\{\rho \circ \chi : \rho \in \operatorname{Hom}^{\mathrm{rat}}(W,X_0)\}$ multiplied on the right by $c$.
--
--   This is the elementary direction of Deuring's isomorphism criterion: the right ideal class of the kernel ideal $\operatorname{Hom}(W,X_0)\chi$ inside the endomorphism ring of $X_0$ depends only on the isomorphism class of the target curve, not on the representative $W$ nor on the chosen isogeny $\chi$. It feeds the comparison of the set of isomorphism classes of elliptic curves admitting an isogeny from $X_0$ with the right class set of the quaternion order, used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of supersingular points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_exists_units_image_kernelIdealSet_eq_image_mul_of_exists_variableChange.lean

